2. Poppler (bundled inside the pdf-docx and pdf-pptx WASM modules)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The `pdf-docx` and `pdf-pptx` conversion modules (the modules that import
PDF files) are built with LibreOffice's `--enable-poppler` option
(`engine/modules/pdf-docx/autogen.input`, `engine/modules/pdf-pptx/autogen.input`)
and additional first-party patches that inline Poppler's PDF parsing into
the WebAssembly build in place of LibreOffice's normal subprocess-based
PDF import (`engine/patches/*-pdfimport-inline-wasm-pdf-docx.patch`,
`engine/patches/*-poppler-emscripten-no-fontconfig-pdf-docx.patch`).
Poppler is therefore bundled inside those two shipped modules.

Poppler is distributed under the GNU General Public License, version 2 or
(at the distributor's option) any later version (GPL-2.0-or-later), per
its own `COPYING` (GPLv2) and `COPYING3` (GPLv3) files. This project does
not modify Poppler's own source beyond the build-configuration/Emscripten
compatibility patches named above.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
2a. Conveyance terms of the pdf-docx and pdf-pptx modules
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Upstream LibreOffice deliberately isolates Poppler in a SEPARATE executable
(`sdext/Executable_xpdfimport.mk`); the core libraries do not link it, and
invoke it as a subprocess over pipes and exit codes. WebAssembly cannot
spawn processes, so this project's patch links Poppler directly into the
`pdfimport` library — the patch says so itself:

    # On Emscripten, we need to link poppler directly since we can't spawn
    # xpdfimport

Poppler and LibreOffice therefore occupy one linear memory space in a single
binary, communicating by direct in-process C++ calls rather than pipes. That
is a combined work, not mere aggregation.

